)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533957 true] [17592186111291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533957 true] [17592186111291 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533957 true] [17592186111347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533957 true] [17592186111347 1145 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its $y$-intercept is $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186268663 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1089 "q-117135" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1100 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1113 #uuid "db5339f3-583c-5717-b07c-231fcf7c1fd9" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268662 1145 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its $y$-intercept is $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962979/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268664 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268665 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268666 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268667 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268668 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1041 "selection" 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186268668 13194139533957 true] [17592186268663 1110 #uuid "216c1358-a4b0-5658-b4da-4b